From fbf4114324bfea8cd27ef40a989944c3745e7d5f Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Mon, 21 Sep 2026 02:55:37 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] Eight Max cape variants, and god capes stop being a list of
 names

Backlog item 2. Combine the Max cape with a Fire cape, an Infernal cape or any of the six god
capes and it takes that cape's look and its stats. OSRS has fourteen variants; eight of them have
a source cape that exists in this build, and the other six do not, so they are left out rather
than invented - Ava's accumulator, the assembler, the Masori assembler, Ardougne cloak 4, the
mythical cape and Dizana's quiver are all absent here.

EVERY NUMBER CAME OUT OF THE OSRS ITEM TABLE, not the wiki's rendering of it. index 2 group 10 of
the newest cache, decoded by tools/models/objconfig474.py: the bonuses, the model ids, the
recolour pairs, the 2d cameras, the weights, the costs and the op lists. The param slots are not
remembered either - three records pin all twelve of them. The plain Max cape gives 5..9 = 9 and
11 = 4, which can only be its +9 defence and +4 prayer; the Fire max cape gives 0..4 = 1 and
10 = 4; the Saradomin max cape gives 3 = 10 and 8 = 10, which can only be magic attack and magic
defence. tools/maxcapevariantspec.json holds the lot and the six wiki pages read beside it.

A VARIANT IS ITS SOURCE CAPE'S STATS. Fire is +1 attack all round, +11 defence, +4 strength, +2
prayer; infernal +4 melee, +12 defence, +8 strength; a god cape +10 magic attack; an imbued one
+15. Battery group 13 compares each variant with its SOURCE RECORD rather than with the spec, and
then with the spec as a second check - the two catch different mistakes. One catches a variant
drifting from its cape; the other catches both drifting together away from OSRS.

TWELVE OF THE SIXTEEN NEED NO NEW MODEL. In OSRS the six god max capes are the plain Max cape's
own model with five recolours, and their hoods are the max hood's with six - so here they point at
obj_max_cape* and obj_max_hood*, which the Max cape round already converted. Only fire and
infernal are real models: four imports, sixteen .ob2 files, all round-trip verified.

THE RECOLOURS ARE THE PART THAT COULD HAVE GONE WRONG SILENTLY. They name source colours on OSRS's
models, and ours are re-encodings. If the re-encoding had moved a colour, every recolour would
match nothing, all six god capes would render as a plain Max cape, and no config, no linter and no
packed-data check would notice: nothing would look broken, it would look like the wrong cape. So
tools/maxvariantrender.py applies each pair through ob2render's own Model.recolour and counts the
faces it hits.

Two pairs are MEANT to hit nothing, and finding that out is why that tool exists. A hood's six-pair
list is the cape's five with one appended - same five sources, in order - and two of them (hsl16
668 and 673) are colours of the cape and of no hood model. Decoding OSRS's own models out of index
7 settled it: same face counts, 416/504/477/698/698 and 646/642/640, and the same colour sets. So
the conversion moved nothing and the two inert pairs are Jagex's. The spec lists them and the tool
asserts exactly that pattern, so a recolour that SHOULD bite and stops biting goes red while those
two stay quiet.

A VARIANT IS NOT A MAX CAPE, which was Corey's choice when asked and is OSRS's rule. It keeps four
"acts as" perks - the Cooks' Guild, the Crafting Guild, essence pouches not degrading, warm
clothing - and the skillcape emote, and nothing else. No Teleports, no Features, no other passive.
The cache agrees and that is better evidence than the wiki's prose: obj 13280 carries four
inventory ops and all eight variants carry only Wear.

It is an ALLOWLIST rather than a blocklist, in the one chokepoint ~skillcape_worn, and "is this a
variant" is maxvariant_source answering something other than null - no list of the eight lives in
that file. So a perk added to skillcape_perks.rs2 later is inherited by the plain Max cape
automatically and by a variant not at all, which is the right way round.

THE GOD CAPES WERE A LIST OF NAMES AND ARE NOW A TABLE. Deciding that a god max cape counts as its
god cape - the wiki's God capes page lists all six as god capes, and the item table gives a variant
the same god param as the plain cape, 40/41/42 - reached further than the spells. god_gear.rs2
named capes in PAIRS: check_conflicting_god_cape(zamorak_cape, guthix_cape). Four capes a god does
not fit in two arguments, so:

  * god_cape.enum maps twelve capes to their god and god_staff_god maps the three staves
  * ~god_worn_cape and ~god_worn_staff answer ^god_none for an empty slot AND for an ordinary
    item, which is what lets one comparison do the whole conflict test
  * ~god_cape_and_staff replaces the condition at nine sites: three god spells in the player copy,
    the same three in the pvp copy, and three battle mages
  * the two pair-at-a-time procs are gone

The strong form of that is a check: no script anywhere still tests for a god cape by name. Adding
a cape is one line in the enum now.

WHAT THEY DID NOT JOIN, and why it is a line and not an oversight: category=armour_godcape drives
"you may only possess one sacred cape at a time" on pickup, and the three plain capes are destroyed
on drop with a lose_cape_message. Those are the Mage Arena's economy for the capes it hands out,
not properties of being a god cape, and OSRS puts neither on a max cape. A variant is untradeable
and is made from a cape you already earned.

THE COMBINE IS ONE TRIGGER, not eight: OpHeldUHandler tries the target item's trigger first and the
used item's second, so [opheldu,max_cape] fires whichever way round the player does it and
maxvariant_cape turns the other item into the answer. The knife half has to go the other way -
eight triggers on the capes - because [opheldu,knife] is already taken by the gnome cooking
fruit-cutting handler and may only be declared once. The hood is required in both directions, and
that is not a courtesy: the Max hood is what becomes the variant's hood, and there is nothing to
turn into a Fire max hood if it is in the bank. The two operations are exact inverses and the
battery checks both halves.

THREE THINGS THE TOOLS CAUGHT BEFORE THE BUILD DID: rs2check said last_item is an obj and not a
namedobj, and that there is no human_craft in all.seq. Then the server compiler said what rs2check
did not - return(~god_worn_staff = $god) is a syntax error, because `=` is a comparison inside an
if and not an expression. Same trap as `$n ! 0` in the Barrows puzzle round, and the compiler is
still the authority.

AND A FINDING OF MINE THAT WAS WRONG, recorded rather than quietly dropped. I claimed both Tzhaar
capes were missing param=rangeattack,1 and "fixed" them. Both already had it. The survey grepped
with param=(stab|slash|crush|magic|ranged|attack|defence|str|prayer), where 'ranged' does not match
'rangeattack' and 'attack' only matches at the start of the value - so the regex could not see the
line on any record and its absence from the output was read as absence from the file.
fightcave_battery.py caught it: it pins the Infernal cape at exactly the bonuses its own spec names
and counts them, and a duplicate made thirteen. Its per-param checks had been printing "param
rangeattack is the cache's 1" as OK the whole time. Both edits are reverted, the spec records it
under a_finding_of_mine_that_was_wrong, and the battery now checks the inverse: no source cape
carries a duplicated param line, which is what that mistake looks like in a config.

FOUR OF MY OWN CHECKS WERE TOO WEAK and five mutations proved it:

  * the four-skill allowlist searched for the four names and passed while a mutation added agility
    beside them - a check that cannot see an extra is checking a subset, not a list;
  * two checks read their own explanatory comments, which quote the exact thing they forbid.
    nocomment() now, and third time in this project;
  * the caller check counted FILES and passed while one of the three calls inside battle_mage.rs2
    was deleted. It counts calls, keyed by PATH - the player and pvp copies of each god spell have
    the same file name;
  * the three god STAFF triggers had no check at all until a mutation deleted one and nothing went
    red.

Also: the harness counted every survivor twice, so a run with five survivors announced ten.

65 mutations, each caught by its own check. tools/mutate_labels.py audits all 65 as matching a
check their own checker prints.

THE GENERATED FILES ARE LF, like every other config in this repo. They were CRLF for one commit,
which git's text=auto would have normalised on the way in and a Windows checkout could have handed
back either way - and genmaxvariants.py --check compared bytes, so it would have reported "would
change" on a tree nobody had touched. The generator writes LF and the check ignores line endings
now, both.

pack/enum.pack is GENERATED - .gitignore has it and deploy_build.sh deletes it before every build -
so nothing is taken there; the ids come out of the .enum files. pack/obj.pack is tracked and an
obj id is part of what a save file means, so those twelve are taken and written down.

